.TH IPINTTEST 1
.SH NAME
ipinttest \- check that IPint arithmetic raises rather than panics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ipinttest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ipinttest
asks
.IR keyring (2)'s
.B IPint
for the arithmetic that the C library underneath it answers with
.IR sysfatal :
a remainder or a division by zero, a modular exponentiation with a
negative exponent or a zero modulus, and an inverse that does not
exist.
.PP
None of those is a bug in the caller's sense.  A modulus arrives from a
certificate, an exponent from a key file, and a base that shares a
factor with the modulus is something arithmetic produces.  But
.B mpdiv
ends
.B "divide by zero"
in
.IR sysfatal ,
and on a native kernel
.I sysfatal
is a panic \(em so one line of Limbo could stop a booted machine.  The
builtins check first and raise an exception the program can catch:
.BR "zero divide" ,
.BR "negative exponent"
or
.BR "no inverse" .
.PP
Six of the calls were fatal before the guards existed; the three
modular operations
.RB ( mulmod ,
.B addmod
and
.BR submod )
are newer than them, and would have been fatal too had they been
written as an operation followed by a
.BR mod .
.PP
Each case is run inside a handler.  A case that returns normally is
reported as a failure; a case that is still fatal takes the test with
it, which is a failure of a louder kind.
.PP
Beside each is a positive control \(em the same operation with legal
arguments, checked against its answer \(em so a build that raised for
everything would fail here rather than pass.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ipinttest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyring-ipint (2),
.IR p256test (1)
.SH BUGS
It covers the calls that were fatal, not the whole of
.BR IPint .
